---
description: Stacked pull requests merge into their base branch, not the default branch; how to land a stack without losing work
alwaysApply: true
---

# Stacked pull requests

- Open a pull request against the default branch whenever it does not need another open pull request's code. A stack is the exception, not the default.
- A stacked pull request merges into its base branch. If the lower pull request merged first, the upper one lands on a branch nobody ships, and the default branch never gets it.
- Retarget the upper pull request to the default branch right after the lower one merges, and before anyone merges the upper one. Retargeting in the same minute as a merge can lose the race.
- After every merge in a stack, check that each commit reached the default branch (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/<default>`). Do not trust "merged" in the pull request event.
- When work landed on a dead stack branch, open one new pull request from that branch to the default branch. Do not cherry-pick or re-push the commits under new hashes.
- Say in the pull request body which pull request it sits on, so the person merging knows the order.
